== The Epistle Dedicatory

#strong[#emph[TO THE RIGHT HONORABLE, RIGHT Worshipful, and my other] beloved parishioners, inhabitants of the precinct #emph[of Blackfriars, LONDON: such] sufficiency of grace #emph[as may bring them to] fullness of glory.]

#smallcaps[If] noble birth, high honor, great wealth, true piety, generous charity, a good esteem of God’s word and ministers, and in particular a whole-hearted love of the author, are reasons for choosing a patron for his work, then I for my part need not go far for a patron. In my own parish are all these. To you#super[+] therefore (Right Honorable, Right Worshipful, and my other beloved parishioners, most worthy of all due respect) I dedicate these poor labors of mine on #emph[domestical duties.] To testify to the equal duty which I owe and the impartial respect which I bear to you#super[+] all, I make you#super[+] all one patron. You#super[+] were the first over whom I ever had any ministerial charge. To this charge I was called by your#super[+] free choice. Among you#super[+] I have spent #footnote[Since Midsummer 1608.] almost two full apprenticeships. You#super[+] have always so accepted my labors and respected my person that I never had any cause to repent of my accepting this place and calling, but rather cause to thank God for it. My desire is (if it may seem good to the divine providence) to spend all my days among you#super[+], and, while I am among you#super[+], to help forward your#super[+] spiritual edification. This is the chief end of my calling. It is the mark I have aimed at, as in the ordinary course of my ministry, so in publishing these #emph[eight treatises of domestical duties] and dedicating them to you#super[+]. As I have preached these duties in your#super[+] hearing, and published them in your#super[+] name, in testimony of love and duty, so do you#super[+] show your#super[+] kind acceptance of my former and my latter labors by conscientiously observing them, so far as they agree with God’s word, so that all who know you#super[+] may know by that clear demonstration how well you#super[+] have relished and approved them. In this way you#super[+] will gain much profit, and I much comfort, by my labors. Oh, if the head and the separate members of a family would each be persuaded to be conscientious in performing their own particular duties, what a sweet society and happy harmony there would be in houses! What excellent nurseries families would be for church and commonwealth! It is necessary that good order be first set in families. For as families came before other states, so they are somewhat more necessary;#footnote[πρότερον καὶ ἀναγκαιότερον οἰκία πόλεως. Arist. Eth. #emph[lib. 8.] cap. #emph[12.]] and good members of a family are likely to make good members of church and commonwealth. The subject matter of these treatises is therefore worth handling, if I were able to handle it according to its worth. I have endeavored to do what I could in it, though I have not attained to what I would. Be like God, who, if there is first a willing mind, accepts according to what a man has, and not according to what he does not have. Though for such a matter as is handled in these treatises the work may seem at first sight too long, yet I hope the observant reader will not find it too tedious. It is the variety of many points, #footnote[Μακρολόγος ἐστὶν ὁ περὶ ὀλίγων πολλὰ λέγων. Πολυλόγος ὁ περὶ πολλῶν πολλά. #emph[Ammon. Multum loqui est rem superfluis agere verbis. Aug. Epist.] 121. #emph[ad Prob.] \[He is long-winded who says much about few things; he is wordy who says much about many. To speak much is to handle a matter with needless words.\]] not the long-windedness of a few, which has made this book swell to the size it has. The first treatise (which is a fourth part of the book) contains a commentary on that part of Scripture out of which #emph[domestical duties] are raised. In it the apostle, setting forth Christ and the church as patterns to husbands and wives, vividly declares the great love of Christ to his church and the near union between them, together with other deep mysteries; and the unfolding of these has kept me a little longer. But as I have dwelt for a while on main matters of great weight, so I have passed very briefly over other points. The other treatises, in which the duties themselves are handled, are each much shorter than the first. In them I have simply set forth and briefly proved the truth and fairness of the separate duties, except some choice points which are of special use, or at least are much questioned through disuse; and these I have handled more fully. And because contraries laid side by side #footnote[παρ’ ἄλληλα τὰ ἐναντία μάλιστα φαίνεται. #emph[Arist. Rhet. lib.] 3. #emph[cap.] 2.] set each other forth in their true colors, I have joined to every duty the contrary fault and straying from it. For many who hear the duties think all is well enough, until they also hear the contrary vices, by which they are most convicted in their consciences. Concerning the many faults and vices of bad husbands, wives, parents, children, masters and servants, censured in these treatises, let me entreat you#super[+] not to apply them too generally to all husbands, wives, parents, children, masters and servants. Has not wise Solomon much censured the flightiness, shrewishness, pride, flattery, and other vices of women? And shall sober, meek, humble, chaste women think themselves censured by it? By the same reasoning unchaste harlots, untrustworthy gossips, restless shrews, and proud dames might think themselves commended by those excellent commendations which he gives of good women. Let every one, as their conscience (an impartial judge) bears them witness, make a right application of everything to themselves. In this way we ministers will be freed from many evil suspicions. I remember that when these #emph[domestical duties] were first delivered from the pulpit, much exception was taken against the application of a wife’s subjection to restraining her from disposing of the common goods of the family without, or against, her husband’s consent. But surely those who took those exceptions did not consider well the #emph[cautions] and #emph[limitations] which were delivered then, and are now expressly noted again. They are these: that the restraint mentioned is not to be extended to the #emph[wife’s own goods,]#footnote[#emph[Paraphernalia.] Treatise 3, §. 21.] no, nor too strictly to such #emph[goods as are set apart for the use of the family,]#footnote[Ibid.] nor to #emph[extraordinary cases,]#footnote[Treatise 3, §. 22.] nor always to an #emph[express consent,]#footnote[Treatise 3, §. 19.] nor to the #emph[consent of such husbands as are incapable, or far and long absent.]#footnote[Treatise 3, §. 18.] If any other warrantable caution is shown to me, I will be as willing to admit it as any of these. Now, so that my meaning may not always be twisted, I pray you#super[+], in reading of the restraint on a wife’s power in disposing of the goods of the family, always bear those cautions in mind. Other exceptions were taken against some other particular duties of wives. For many who can hear their duties declared in general terms patiently enough cannot bear to hear those generals illustrated in their particular branches. This comes too near the quick, and pierces too deep. But (to interpret all according to the rule of love, in the better sense) I take the main reason for the many exceptions which were taken to be this. Wives’ duties were handled in the first place (according to the apostle’s method), and so it was taught (as it must have been taught, unless the truth were to be betrayed) what a wife is bound to, in the uttermost extent of that subjection under which God has put her, in case her husband will insist on the uttermost of his authority. This was taken as if I had taught that a husband might and ought to exact the uttermost, and that a wife was bound, in that uttermost extent, to do all that was delivered as duty, whether her husband exacted it or not. But when I came to deliver husbands’ duties, #footnote[Treatise 4, §. 18.] I showed that he ought not to exact whatever his wife was bound to (in case it were exacted by him), but that he ought to make her a joint governor of the family with himself, and leave the ordering of many things to her discretion, and carry himself toward her with all honorable and kind respect. In a word, I have so set down a husband’s duties that, if he is wise and conscientious in observing them, his wife can have no just cause to complain of her subjection. What makes a wife’s yoke heavy and hard is a husband’s abuse of his authority, and his pressing his wife’s duty more than performing his own, which is directly contrary to the #footnote[Eph 5:33. See Treatise 1, §. 95.] apostle’s rule. I have been forced to make this just defense so that I might not forever be judged (as some have censured me) #footnote[μισογύνης. Athen.] #emph[a hater of women.] Now, so that I might give some ease in all those places where a wife’s yoke may seem to pinch most, I have made a reference, in the margin beside every head of wives’ duties, to the duties of husbands that answer to it, and marked the reference with this sign \*, so that it may be turned to more readily. Indeed, I have further set side by side, and laid evenly one against another in one view, the heads of husbands’ and wives’ duties, as they answer each other, and in the same way the contrary failings, with a reference to the particular sections where they are handled. So on the one side it may appear that, if both are conscientious and careful to perform their own duty, the yoke of marriage will lie so evenly on both their necks that the wife will be pinched by it no more than the husband, but it will be like Christ’s spiritual yoke, #emph[light] and #emph[easy;]#footnote[Matt 11:30.] and on the other side it may be clear that there is commonly as much failing by husbands in their duties as by wives in theirs. This parallel, setting out evenly the duties of each and the contrary failings, I have placed next to this epistle. And I have further added to it a table of the separate heads of the points that are handled in the eight following treatises, so that by this help you#super[+] may more readily find such particular points as you#super[+] most especially desire to read. To show that the duties prescribed to husbands, wives, parents, children, masters, and servants are such as they are bound to in conscience, I have endeavored to show how they are grounded on the word of God and gathered from it. To avoid long-windedness, I have put most of the quotations in the margin. If your#super[+] leisure allows, you#super[+] would do well to search them out. Two things have been special helps to me for finding out the many duties noted in these treatises, and the vices contrary to them: #emph[observation] and #emph[disposition.] #emph[Observation,] both of such duties as the Scripture commends and such contrary vices as it condemns, and also of such commendable virtues as I liked well in those husbands, wives, parents, children, masters, and servants among whom I came, and such unseemly vices as I disliked in them; and #emph[disposition] of one point after another in the best order that I could. My method and manner of proceeding brought many things to my mind which otherwise might have slipped by. For by method many separate points belonging to one matter are drawn out, as in a chain one link draws up another. There is no better way to find many observations in a text than by a methodical analysis of it. As method is a help to #emph[invention,] so also to #emph[retention.] It is like the thread or wire on which pearls are strung, which keeps them from scattering. And if a man is cast into a labyrinth by an abundance of matter, by the help of method he may easily and readily find his way out again. In these respects #emph[method] is fitly called the #footnote[Methodus intelligentiae parens est, & magistra memoriae.] #emph[mother of the mind] and #emph[mistress of memory.] If you#super[+] mark well the order of the points and how one depends on another, you#super[+] will find as great a help in understanding and remembering #footnote[#emph[Ordo memoriae maximè, lumen affert. Cic.] 2. #emph[de Orat.] \[Order most of all brings light to the memory.\]] them as I did in finding them out and setting them in order.

Because there is no one word that includes both masters and mistresses under it, as fathers and mothers are included under #emph[parents,] and sons and daughters under #emph[children,] I have, according to the Scripture’s way of speaking, included mistresses under masters, so that the duties enjoined to #emph[masters] belong to #emph[mistresses,] so far as may stand with their sex. To conclude, in recompense for all my labors I heartily pray you#super[+] all to pray heartily for him who daily prays for you#super[+], even #footnote[Church-court in Blackfriars, London. 2 Feb. 1621.]

